          %ul
            %li
              Every object can be written as a monoidal product of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \ge 0
              copies of the “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              \-cube”.
          %p
            Often one asks that the monoidal structure is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+monoidal+category"} strict
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PRO"} PRO
          %ul
            %li
              automorphisms of cubes, such as
              %em permutations/symmetries
              or
              = succeed ";" do
                %em reversals
            %li
              additional face-like maps, such as
              = succeed ";" do
                %em diagonals
            %li
              additional degeneracy-like maps, such as
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+cubical+set"}
                  %em connections
          %p
            Often we describe a cube category as generated by some subset of these morphisms. Typically there is a functor from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            sending the
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]^n
          %p
            A presheaf on a category of cubes is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical set
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            A uniform way to present many cube categories is as free (strict) monoidal categories with some structure, i.e., as classifying categories for monoidal theories (
            %a{:href => "#Mauri2005"}> Mauri 2005
            ). This approach is described by
            %a{:href => "#GrandisMauri2003"} Grandis and Mauri (2003)
            and expanded upon by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BuchholtzMorehouse2017"} Buchholtz and Morehouse (2017)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rule"} structural rules
          %ul
            %li
              Degeneracies (always included) correspond to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicartesian+monoidal+category"} requiring the monoidal unit to be terminal
            %li
              Permutations correspond to requiring the monoidal product to be
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %li
              Diagonals correspond to requiring a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category+with+diagonals"} monoidal category with diagonals
          %p
            Including permutations thus makes the cube category a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PROP"} PROP
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
          %p
            The construction of the classifying category can be unpacked into an explicit description of a cube category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            in terms of generating morphisms and relations (the “cubical identities”). Most cube categories can also be described as wide subcategories of the full subcategory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            consisting of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}^n
            for
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \ge 0
          %p
            At least in the cartesian case, the classifying category description implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            is equivalent to the opposite of the category of free finitely-generated algebras for the theory. The 1-cube
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^1
            can often be cast as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
          %h3#the_ordered_cube_category The ordered cube category
          %p
            The
            %strong ordered cube category
            or
            %strong restricted cube category
            (
            %a{:href => "#GrandisMauri2003"}> Grandis &amp; Mauri 2003, Section 4
            ) is the minimal cube category, generated by face and degeneracy maps. When unqualified,
            %strong cube category
            usually refers to this category, which historically was the first to be defined.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong ordered cube category
              is the free strict monoidal category with terminal unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Box,\otimes,I^0)
              equipped with an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{1}
              and two maps
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_0,\delta_1 : I^{0} \to I^{1}
          .num_defn
            %h6#notation Notation
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
              be an integer. We denote the object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %munder
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi I
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo ⊗
                          %msup
                            %mi I
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ⏟
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{n}{\underbrace{I^{1} \otimes \cdots \otimes I^{1}}}
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{n}
          .num_defn
            %h6#terminology Terminology
            %p
              In addition to the maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_0
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi α
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n \otimes \delta_\alpha \otimes I^m
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi m
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n,m \ge 0
              are also called
              = succeed "." do
                %em faces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n \otimes \epsilon \otimes I^m
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi m
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n,m \ge 0
              are also called degeneracies.
          %p
            See (
            %a{:href => "#GrandisMauri2003"}> Grandis &amp; Mauri 2003, Section 4
            ) for the following two propositions.
          .num_defn
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p The ordered cube category is the classifying category of the monoidal theory of bipointed objects with the weakening structural rule.
          #OrderedCubesInSet.num_defn
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The ordered cube category is isomorphic to the subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              whose objects are powers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^n
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 = \{0, 1\}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{n} \to 2^{n-1}
              which delete a coordinate and face maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{n} \to 2^{n+1}
              which insert a 0 or 1 without modifying the order of coordinates. The monoidal product is the restriction of the cartesian product on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi δ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi δ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^0, \delta^1 \colon 1 \to 2
              and the basic degeneracy is the map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \colon 2 \to 1
          .num_defn
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              It follows from Proposition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OrderedCubesInSet"}
              that every morphism in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              can be written as a finite composite of maps of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi α
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n \otimes \delta_\alpha \otimes I^m
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mi n
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n \otimes \epsilon \otimes I^m
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentation+of+a+category+by+generators+and+relations"} generators and relations
              is in section 2 of
            %ul
              %li
                Sjoerd Crans,
                %em
                  Pasting schemes for the monoidal biclosed structure on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                        %mo −
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega-Cat
                (
                %a{:href => "http://crans.fol.nl/papers/thten.html"}> web
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://crans.fol.nl/papers/thten.ps.gz"} ps
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/thten.pdf", :title => ""} pdf
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo □
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≤
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_{\leq 1}
              for the full subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              consisting of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^0
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^1
          .num_defn
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo □
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≤
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_{\leq 1}
              is isomorphic to the category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≤
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_{\leq 1}
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The full subcategory of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
                  on the objects
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [1]
              %li
                %p (A skeleton of) the category of linearly ordered sets of cardinality 1 or 2.
              %li
                %p
                  The indexing category for
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+coequalizer"} reflexive equalizers
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              itself can be recovered as the
              %span.newWikiWord
                free strict monoidal category
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/free+strict+monoidal+category"} ?
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo □
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≤
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \square_{\leq 1}
              whose unit object is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{0}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              is
              %em not
              the free strict monoidal category on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≤
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \square_{\leq 1}
              %em with specified unit
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≤
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \square_{\leq 1}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{0}
          %h3#cubes_with_connections Cubes with connections
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in \{0,1\}
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              \-connection
            in a cube category is a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_\alpha \colon I^1 \otimes I^1 \to I^1
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (I^1,\delta_\alpha,\gamma_\alpha)
            defines a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+in+a+monoidal+category"} monoid in a monoidal category
            and additionally the equations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi α
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi α
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi α
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi α
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi α
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\gamma_\alpha \circ (\delta_{1-\alpha} \otimes I^1) = \gamma_\alpha \circ (\delta_{1-\alpha} \otimes I^1) = \delta_{1-\alpha} \circ \epsilon
          %p
            hold. Often these are called negative and positive connections rather than
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "-connections" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]^2 \to [0,1]
            sending a pair of points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)
            to their maximum, likewise the positive connection as the minimum. We may more generally refer to maps of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi α
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n \otimes \gamma_\alpha \otimes I^m
            as connections.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong
                ordered cube category with an
                = succeed "-connection" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              is the free strict monoidal category with terminal unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Box,\otimes,I^0)
              equipped with an object
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{1}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_0,\delta_1 : I^{0} \to I^{1}
              = succeed "-connection." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %p
            We can also consider the cube category with both negative and positive connections. The cube category has several advantages over the minimal cube category as a shape for higher structures; see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+cubical+set"} connection on a cubical set
            for more information.
          %h3#symmetric_cube_categories Symmetric cube categories
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong symmetric cube category
              is the free strict
              %em symmetric
              monoidal category with terminal unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Box,\otimes,I^0)
              equipped with an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{1}
              and two maps
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_0,\delta_1 : I^{0} \to I^{1}
          %p
            The symmetric cube category is also called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicartesian+monoidal+category"} semicartesian
            cube category, as well as the “BCH” cube category after its use by
            %a{:href => "#BezemCoquandHuber2013"} Bezem, Coquand, &amp; Huber (2013)
            to interpret
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            constructively. It is also used in the semantics of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parametric+dependent+type+theory"} internally parametric dependent type theory
          %p
            The symmetric monoidal structure induces an automorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi σ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{\sigma} \colon I^n \cong I^n
            for each element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in \Sigma_n
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements. The symmetric cube category can be compared with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+simplicial+set"} symmetric simplex category
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+category"} cyclic category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            = succeed "-simplex," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p The symmetric cube category admits the following alternative description, used by Bezem, Coquand, and Huber:
          .num_defn
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              The symmetric cube category is equivalent to the category whose objects are finite sets and whose morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon I \to J
              are set functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi J
                    %mo →
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon J \to I \sqcup \{0,1\}
              such that the restriction of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              to the preimage of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is injective.
          .num_defn
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical set
              over the symmetric cube category can also be seen as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nominal+set"} nominal set
              with additional structure; see
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Pitts2015"} Pitts (2015)
          .num_defn
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              %a{:href => "#Isaacson2011"} Isaacson (2011)
              uses a cube category with symmetries and a positive connection, while
              %a{:href => "#GrandisMauri2003"} Grandis and Mauri (2003)
              describe a cube category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
              with symmetries and both positive and negative connections. Grandis and Mauri also take the connections to be symmetric, making
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi α
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (I^1,\delta_\alpha,\gamma_\alpha)
              are
              %em commutative
              monoids for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in \{0,1\}
          .num_defn
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              %a{:href => "#KrishnanRudman2025"} Krishnan and Rudman (2025)
              consider the subcategory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pos"} Pos
              whose objects are
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0 \lt 1\}^n
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \ge 0
              and whose morphisms are the monotone maps that preserve
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intervals"} intervals
              (in the sense of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+theory"} order theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y,z) \mapsto (x \wedge y) \vee (x \wedge z) \vee (y \wedge z)
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#KrishnanRudman2025"} (Krishnan and Rudman 2025, Example 3.13)
          %h3#cubes_with_reversals Cubes with reversals
          %p
            A
            %strong reversal
            or
            %strong reversion
            in a cube category is a involution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \colon I^1 \to I^1
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi α
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \circ \delta_{\alpha} = \delta_{1-\alpha}
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in \{0,1\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 - t
          .num_defn
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              %a{:href => "#GrandisMauri2003"} Grandis and Mauri (2003, Section 9)
              consider a cube category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !\mathbb{K}
              generated by connections, symmetries, and reversals (on top of faces and degeneracies). They also impose laws governing the interaction between reversals and connections,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi γ
                        %mi α
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi γ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo −
                          %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_\alpha \circ (\rho \otimes \rho) = \rho \circ \gamma_{1-\alpha}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi min
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} min
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi max
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} max
          %h3#cartesian_cube_categories Cartesian cube categories
          %p
            By adding diagonal morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n \to I^n \otimes I^n
            on top of the symmetric cube category, we arrive at the cartesian cube category.
          .num_defn
            %h6#remark_7 Remark
            %p
              Symmetries can already be derived from the combination of degeneracies and diagonals
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n \to I^n \otimes I^n
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Mauri2005"} (Mauri 2005, Corollary 5.5)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^1 \to I^1 \otimes I^1
          #CartesianCubes.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong cartesian cube category
              is the free strict
              %em cartesian
              monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Box,\otimes,I^0)
              equipped with an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{1}
              and two maps
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_0,\delta_1 : I^{0} \to I^{1}
          %p
            The cartesian cube category is thus the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bi-pointed+object"} bi-pointed objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone-type duality
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^1
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            itself admits an algebraic description:
          .num_defn
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              The cartesian cube category is isomorphic to the subcategory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              consisting of maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0 \lt 1\}^{n} \to \{0 \lt 1\}^{m}
              preserving binary
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi max
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} max
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi min
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} min
          %p
            Cartesian cube categories (with or without additional structure) are used in the semantics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#CoquandDiagonals2014"} Coquand (2014)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#CohenCoquandHuberMoertberg2017"} Cohen, Coquand, Huber &amp; Mörtberg, 2015
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CartesianCubes"}
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ABCFHL"} (Angiuli, Brunerie, Coquand, Harper, Favonia &amp; Licata 2021)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BuchholtzMorehouse2017"} Buchholtz and Morehouse (2017)
          %h4#cartesian_cubes_with_connections Cartesian cubes with connections
          %p
            In the cartesian regime, there is a proliferation of options for the laws governing the interaction of operations such as connections or reversals. For example, one may ask that the positive and negative connections distribute over each other, a condition which only makes sense in the presence of diagonals. The following two choices, for one and two connections respectively, reflect the equational theory of the topological
            = succeed "-cubes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi min
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} min
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi max
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} max
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong join (resp. meet) semilattice category
              is the Lawvere theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/01-bounded"} 01-bounded
              join (resp. meet)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattice"} semilattices
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong distributive lattice cube category
              (or
              = succeed ")" do
                %strong Dedekind cube category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattices
          %p
            Like the cartesian cube category, these have alternate characterizations via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone-type dualities
            (and in particular
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Birkhoff+duality"} Birkhoff duality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^1
          .num_defn
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              The distributive lattice cube category is isomorphic to the full subcategory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pos"} Pos
              consisting of the powers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0 \lt 1\}^n
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \ge 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0 \lt 1\}^n \to \{0 \lt 1\}^m
              preserving binary joins.
          %p
            Unlike the previously discussed cube categories, the cartesian cube categories with one or more connections are not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+category"} Cauchy complete
          #CartesianConnectionCauchyCompletion.num_defn
            %h6#proposition_6 Proposition
            %p
              The Cauchy completion of the distributive lattice cube category is equivalent to the full subcategory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pos"} Pos
              consisting of finite (bounded) lattices. The Cauchy completion of the join semilattice cube category is equivalent to the subcategory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pos"} Pos
              consisting of finite (bounded) distributive lattices and morphisms preserving binary joins.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Sattler2019"}> Sattler 2019
              ) and (
              %a{:href => "#CavalloSattler2022"}> Cavallo &amp; Sattler (2022), Theorem 4.46
              ).
          .num_defn
            %h6#remark_8 Remark
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mo □
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \to \overline{\Box}
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
              into the Cauchy completions of the semilattice and distributive lattice cube categories, corresponding to the inclusion of linear orders in the categories of Proposition
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CartesianConnectionCauchyCompletion"}
          %h4#cartesian_cubes_with_connections_and_reversals Cartesian cubes with connections and reversals
          %p
            %a{:href => "#BuchholtzMorehouse2017"} Buchholtz and Morehouse (2017)
            consider the cube categories with symmetries, diagonals, connections, and reversals (on top of faces and degenerecies) given by the Lawvere theories of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+algebras"} De Morgan algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleene+algebras"} Kleene algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
          %p
            The Kleene cube category is the choice that makes the evident functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box \to Top
            faithful. The De Morgan cube category is used in the model of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#CohenCoquandHuberMoertberg2017"} Cohen, Coquand, Huber &amp; Mörtberg (2015)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _+
            of nonempty finite sets, so the category of Boolean cubical sets is equivalent to the category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+sets"} symmetric sets
          %h2#as_shapes_for_higher_structures As shapes for higher structures
          %h3#as_test_categories As test categories
          %p
            Many (perhaps all) of the cube categories described above are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+categories"} test categories
            (
            %a{:href => "#BuchholtzMorehouse2017"}> Buchholtz &amp; Morehouse 2017
            ). Hence their categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+categories"} strict test categories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+sets"} model structure on cubical sets
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+cubical+set"} connection on a cubical set
            for more details.
          %h3#tensor_products Tensor products
          %p
            Among all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shapes+for+higher+structures"} geometric shapes for higher structures
            cubes are best suited for describing Gray-like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor products
            of higher structures: there is geometrically obvious way in which to combine the
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{n}
            and the
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{m}
            to the
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+m)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{n} \otimes I^m := I^{n+m}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical sets
            and then on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-category"} strict omega-categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Crans-Gray+tensor+product"} Crans-Gray tensor product
          %h2#reedy_and_eilenbergzilber_structures Reedy and Eilenberg–Zilber structures
          %p
            Many (but not all) cube categories can be seen as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+categories"} Reedy categories
            (or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Reedy+categories"} generalized Reedy categories
            if they contain non-trivial automorphisms), with the degree of an cube given by its dimension. The Reedy face maps are the cubical faces and diagonals (if present), while the Reedy degeneracy maps are the cubical degeneracies and connections (if present).
          %p
            Those cube categories which are Reedy categories are generally also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elegant+Reedy+categories"} elegant Reedy categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Zilber+categories"} Eilenberg-Zilber categories
            if they contain automorphisms.
            %a{:href => "#Campion2023"} Campion (2023)
            enumerates a collection of cube categories and determines which of them are Eilenberg–Zilber categories.
          .num_defn
            %h6#proposition_7 Proposition
            %p The cube categories without diagonals (i.e., having possibly symmetries, connections, or reversals on top of faces and degeneracies) are Eilenberg–Zilber categories.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Campion2023"}> Campion 2023, Corollary 7.10
              ).
          %p
            One should check Campion for the precise definition of cube category used there. The cube category of
            %a{:href => "#KrishnanRudman2025"} Krishnan and Rudman (2025)
            is the largest Eilenberg–Zilber category among the concrete (i.e., given by a subcategory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            with objects
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}^n
          %p Cube categories with diagonals are more delicate.
          .num_defn
            %h6#proposition_8 Proposition
            %p
              The cartesian cube category (of
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CartesianCubes"}
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Campion2023"}> Campion 2023, Theorem 8.12(1)
              ).
          %p
            A Reedy category is always
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+category"} Cauchy complete
          .num_defn
            %h6#proposition_9 Proposition
            %p The Cauchy completion of the semilattice cube category admits no Reedy structure.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#CavalloSattler2022"}> Cavallo &amp; Sattler 2022, Proposition A.1
              ).
          .num_defn
            %h6#proposition_10 Proposition
            %p The Cauchy completion of the distributive lattice cube category is not an Eilenberg–Zilber category.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Campion2023"}> Campion 2023, Theorem 8.12(2)
              ).
          .num_defn
            %h6#remark_9 Remark
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#CavalloSattler2022"}> Cavallo &amp; Sattler 2022
              ) develops a generalization of the theory of EZ-categories which can be used to work with the semilattice cube category, but this theory does not apply to the distributive lattice cube category (
              %a{:href => "#CavalloSattler2022"}> Cavallo &amp; Sattler 2022, Appendix A.2
              ).
          .num_defn
            %h6#proposition_11 Proposition
            %p The Cauchy completion of the Boolean cube category is an Eilenberg–Zilber category.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_6 Proof
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Campion2023"}> Campion 2023, Theorem 8.12(3)
              ).
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"}> simplex category
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globe+category"} globe category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cell+category"} cell category
          %p
            See also the related entries at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical set
          %h2#references References
          %ul
